---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

// Tool pages land in M1, each at its own indexable URL. Listed here as the routes that
// will exist, so the shape of the site is visible from the start.
//
// DELIBERATELY NOT LINKS UNTIL THE PAGE EXISTS. An earlier draft of this page linked each
// name to `/{slug}`. None of those routes is built yet, so every link would have been a
// 404 — on the one page whose argument is that it tells you the truth about itself. Each
// entry gains its link in the pull request that ships its page, starting with merge.
const plannedTools = [
  { name: "Merge PDF", blurb: "Combine several PDFs into one, in the order you choose." },
  { name: "Split PDF", blurb: "Take a range of pages out into their own document." },
  { name: "Rotate PDF", blurb: "Turn pages that were scanned the wrong way up." },
  { name: "Reorder PDF", blurb: "Move pages around without losing any of them." },
  { name: "Compress PDF", blurb: "Make a PDF smaller to send or store." },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="burrow — file tools that run on your own computer"
  description="Free, open-source PDF tools that run in your browser. Your files are never uploaded, because there is no server to upload them to."
>
  <div class="read">
    <h1>Your files stay on this computer.</h1>

    <p>
      burrow is a set of PDF tools that run inside your browser. When you open a file here, it is
      read by code running on your own machine. It is never sent anywhere, because there is nowhere
      to send it: burrow is a static site with no server that could receive a file.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    /*
    THE HERO IS THE BOUNDARY, DRAWN. Not an illustration standing in for evidence — it
    names the real pieces a file passes through and the one thing that does not happen to
    it.

    IT IS MARKUP, NOT AN SVG, AND THAT WAS A CORRECTION. The first version was an inline
    <svg>, chosen because `img-src 'self'` admits no `data:` URI (ADR 0014) and a separate
    .svg would be a second request. Both true, and both beside the point: text inside an
    SVG scales with the drawing, so at 390px the whole diagram — the most important thing
    on the page — rendered at about 6px and could not be read. Screenshotting it is how
    that was found.

    As markup it reflows: the row becomes a column, the dashed boundary moves from the side
    to the top, and every word is real text at the reader's own font size, selectable and
    translatable. The <ol> is deliberate — a file really does pass through these in order —
    but the numbers are not shown, because a three-item flow does not need counting.
  */
  }
  <figure class="boundary">
    <div class="boundary__here">
      <p class="boundary__label">this computer</p>
      <ol class="boundary__steps" role="list">
        <li><b>your file</b><span>stays here</span></li>
        <li><b>burrow</b><span>WebAssembly</span></li>
        <li><b>the result</b><span>you save it</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="boundary__elsewhere">
      <p class="boundary__label">everywhere else</p>
      <p class="boundary__nothing">nothing</p>
      <p class="boundary__note">no upload, no account, no record of what you open</p>
    </div>

    <figcaption>
      Nothing crosses the dashed line. The page itself is downloaded from a server, like any page
      &mdash; that happens before you choose a file, and nothing is requested afterwards.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="read">
    <h2>What works today</h2>

    <p>
      Nothing yet, and this page will say so until that changes. burrow is being built in the open
      and is at the stage where the engines run in the browser and the safety work around them is
      done; the tools themselves are next. There is no sign-up to wait on — when a tool page is
      here, it works.
    </p>

    <h2>The tools being built</h2>

    <dl class="tools">
      {
        plannedTools.map((tool) => (
          <div class="tools__row">
            <dt>{tool.name}</dt>
            <dd>{tool.blurb}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2>How you can check any of this</h2>

    <p>
      Open your browser's developer tools, go to the network tab, and use burrow. After the page and
      its engines have loaded, you will see no further requests — not while a file is open, not when
      one finishes. The same thing is asserted on every commit, in three browsers, by a test that
      reads the web server's own log rather than the browser's report of it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The code is <a href="https://github.com/TensorGreed/burrow">public</a>, and so is the
      reasoning behind it: the architecture decisions are written down, including the ones that
      turned out to be wrong.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Page-specific layout only. Everything else is `src/styles/base.css`. */

  /* The boundary diagram. A row of steps inside a bordered area, with a dashed edge
     separating it from what is outside. Below --bp-stack the row becomes a column and the
     dashed edge moves to the top, because a three-across diagram at 390px is six-point
     type. */
  .boundary {
    margin: var(--space-7) 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    max-width: var(--track-readout);
  }

  .boundary__here {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--edge);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .boundary__label {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .boundary__steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    /* A sequence semantically, but counting to three helps nobody read it.
       `role="list"` is on the element because WebKit strips the list role from a list whose
       `list-style` is `none` -- so the ordering semantics, which are the whole reason this
       is an <ol>, would be lost in exactly the assistive technology where they mattered. */
    list-style: none;
  }

  .boundary__steps li {
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .boundary__steps b {
    display: block;
  }

  .boundary__steps span {
    display: block;
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .boundary__elsewhere {
    /* The dashed edge IS the claim. Stacked, it sits above; side by side, it sits left. */
    padding-block-start: var(--space-4);
    border-block-start: 2px dashed var(--edge);
  }

  /* The one coloured value on the page, and it is the honest one: how much of your file
     leaves this computer. */
  .boundary__nothing {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    color: var(--signal);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: var(--weight-strong);
  }

  .boundary__note {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 22ch;
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .boundary figcaption {
    max-width: var(--track-read);
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  /* 46rem is `--bp-stack`. CSS cannot interpolate a custom property into a media query, so
     the number is repeated here; keep the two in sync. When a second page needs this
     breakpoint, make it a checked assertion rather than a comment. */
  @media (min-width: 46rem) {
    .boundary {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
      align-items: stretch;
    }

    .boundary figcaption {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }

    .boundary__steps {
      grid-auto-flow: column;
      grid-auto-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .boundary__elsewhere {
      padding-block-start: 0;
      padding-inline-start: var(--space-5);
      border-block-start: none;
      border-inline-start: 2px dashed var(--edge);
    }
  }

  .tools {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0 0;
  }

  .tools__row {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0 var(--space-5);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .tools__row dt {
    font-weight: var(--weight-strong);
  }

  .tools__row dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-quiet);
  }

  @media (min-width: 34rem) {
    .tools__row {
      grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
